{"name":"Light Lamb’s Ears","hex":"#d6d9cb","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"light","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye (about 400–750 nanometers): visible light.","Electromagnetic radiation in the wavelength range visible to the human eye or in nearby ranges (infrared or ultraviolet radiation)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To start (a fire).","To set fire to; to set burning."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/light"},{"term":"lamb","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A young sheep; especially, one younger than nine months.","A young goat; a kid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of a sheep, to give birth.","To assist (sheep) to give birth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lamb"},{"term":"ears","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of ear"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of ear"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ears"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ear","blocks":[{"text":"In vertebrates, an ear is the organ that enables hearing and body balance using the vestibular system. In humans, the ear is described as having three parts: the outer ear, the middle ear and the inner ear. The outer ear consists of the auricle, the visible outer part, and the ear canal. The middle ear includes the tympanic cavity and the three ossicles. The inner ear sits in the bony labyrinth, and contains structures which are key to several senses: the semicircular canals, which enable balance and eye tracking when moving; the utricle and saccule, which enable balance when stationary; and the cochlea, which enables hearing. The ear canal is cleaned via earwax, which naturally migrates to the auricle."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Human_right_ear_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Human_right_ear_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ear","wikidata":"Q7362"},{"title":"Light","blocks":[{"text":"Light, visible light, or visible radiation is electromagnetic radiation that can be perceived by the human eye. Visible light spans the visible spectrum and is usually defined as having wavelengths in the range of 400–700 nanometers (nm), corresponding to frequencies of 750–420 terahertz. The visible band sits adjacent to the infrared and the ultraviolet, called collectively optical radiation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Prism_flat_rainbow_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light","wikidata":"Q9128"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/1871_Descent_F937.1_fig03.jpg/120px-1871_Descent_F937.1_fig03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/1871_Descent_F937.1_fig03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/1871_Descent_F937.1_fig03.jpg/120px-1871_Descent_F937.1_fig03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1871_Descent_F937.1_fig03.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Man_able_to_move_him_ear_8jo_WATANABE_Hachijo.gif/330px-Man_able_to_move_him_ear_8jo_WATANABE_Hachijo.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Man_able_to_move_him_ear_8jo_WATANABE_Hachijo.gif/330px-Man_able_to_move_him_ear_8jo_WATANABE_Hachijo.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Man able to wiggle his ear","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMan_able_to_move_him_ear_8jo_WATANABE_Hachijo.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Blausen_0330_EarAnatomy_MiddleEar.png/500px-Blausen_0330_EarAnatomy_MiddleEar.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Blausen_0330_EarAnatomy_MiddleEar.png/1280px-Blausen_0330_EarAnatomy_MiddleEar.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Blausen_0330_EarAnatomy_MiddleEar.png/500px-Blausen_0330_EarAnatomy_MiddleEar.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The middle ear","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlausen_0330_EarAnatomy_MiddleEar.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Blausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar.png/500px-Blausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Blausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar.png/1280px-Blausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Blausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar.png/500px-Blausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The outer ear receives sound, transmitted through the ossicles of the middle ear to the inner ear, where it is converted to a nervous signal in the cochlea and transmitted along the vestibulocochlear nerve.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlausen_0329_EarAnatomy_InternalEar.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/G1._Otic_placode_%28V07a%29.png/330px-G1._Otic_placode_%28V07a%29.png?utm_source=en.wikiped